@meta
id: 05-04
title: Drill — append to the word
chapter: 05
type: drill
skills: word-e
par: 12
minutes: 4
reps: 10
vscode: click after the identifier, type
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
Ten times: make `customerId` optional by putting a `?` after it. Down two
lines, end of the word, append, out, save.

There is nothing to work out. `ea` should stop being two keys and start
being one movement of the hand.

@keys
| `e` | end of the word |
| `a` | append after the cursor |
| `2j` | down two lines |

@hints
`2j` then `e` lands on the `d` of `customerId` — `e` from the indent runs
to the end of the first word on the line.
--
Do not look at the screen between `e` and `a`. If you are checking where
you landed, the drill has not worked yet.

@solution
    2j  ea?<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Ten keystrokes, four of which are the save.

The reason this is worth drilling rather than reading: appending to the
end of a word is one of the two or three most common edits in typed code.
Optional fields, plural names, a trailing comma on the last property. If
`ea` is automatic, all of those are free.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
};

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId?: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
};
